      "writtenOn": "2021-09-29T21:40:29Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "1) Sure, I can change to only revert the MAX_DRM_OPEN_COUNT part in drm_file.c.\n\n2) In gfxstream Vulkan driver, we will open one fd for every host visible vkAllocateMemory(and we will try to reuse opened fd if that memory is vkFreeMemory\u0027ed), I am not sure if we could decide a reasonable limit for that. We can use a different way to manage the mapped memory: use the drm gem object instead of a rendernode fd to manage the underlying resources(see https://r.android.com/1836503), but the AEMU team thinks that removing the limit in the kernel is an easier fix. WDYT?",
      "parentUuid": "ffbf3be9_e2b6f55e",
      "revId": "a59aa797bac85b1860b731179a0d0674e63d195d",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "33092c38_a2023c23",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1293738
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-09-29T21:55:00Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "There\u0027s a per-process fd limit anyway, so this is just deciding on a subset of a pre-existing limit. Also, fds are shared with other activities of the process, so there\u0027s no way to know how many Vulkan will get for any given app.\n\nI think the fuzzing problem was that fuzzers could create so many opens that the system ran out of memory before the fd limit was reached, and then became unstable. Could we not just set this to something more reasonable, like 4096, and call it a day? 128 certainly seems way too low.",
      "parentUuid": "f7b11a85_a9bbca1b",
      "revId": "a59aa797bac85b1860b731179a0d0674e63d195d",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "28a67f87_20f344a0",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
